402-## Adding custom utilities
403-
404-### Simple utilities
405-
406-In addition to using the utilities that ship with Tailwind, you can also add your own custom utilities. This can be useful when there's a CSS feature you'd like to use in your project that Tailwind doesn't include utilities for out of the box.
407-
408:Use the `@utility` directive to add a custom utility to your project:
409-
410-```css
411-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
412:@utility content-auto {
413-  content-visibility: auto;
414-}
415-```
416-
417-You can now use this utility in your HTML:
418-
--
439-### Complex utilities
440-
441-If your custom utility is more complex than a single class name, use nesting to define the utility:
442-
443-```css
444-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
445:@utility scrollbar-hidden {
446-  &::-webkit-scrollbar {
447-    display: none;
448-  }
449-}
450-```
451-
452-### Functional utilities
453-
454:In addition to registering simple utilities with the `@utility` directive, you can also register functional utilities that accept an argument:
455-
456-```css
457-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
458:@utility tab-* {
459-  /* prettier-ignore */
460-  tab-size: --value(--tab-size-*);
461-}
462-```
463-
464-The special `--value()` function is used to resolve the utility value.
--
472-@theme {
473-  --tab-size-2: 2;
474-  --tab-size-4: 4;
475-  --tab-size-github: 8;
476-}
477-
478:@utility tab-* {
479-  /* [!code highlight:2] */
480-  /* prettier-ignore */
481-  tab-size: --value(--tab-size-*);
482-}
483-```
484-
--
487-#### Bare values
488-
489-To resolve the value as a bare value, use the `--value({type})` syntax, where `{type}` is the data type you want to validate the bare value as:
490-
491-```css
492-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
493:@utility tab-* {
494-  tab-size: --value(integer);
495-}
496-```
497-
498-This will match utilities like `tab-1` and `tab-76`.
499-
--
502-#### Literal values
503-
504-To support literal values, use the `--value('literal')` syntax (notice the quotes):
505-
506-```css
507-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
508:@utility tab-* {
509-  tab-size: --value("inherit", "initial", "unset");
510-}
511-```
512-
513-This will match utilities like `tab-inherit`, `tab-initial`, and `tab-unset`.
514-
515-#### Arbitrary values
516-
517-To support arbitrary values, use the `--value([{type}])` syntax (notice the square brackets) to tell Tailwind which types are supported as an arbitrary value:
518-
519-```css
520-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
521:@utility tab-* {
522-  tab-size: --value([integer]);
523-}
524-```
525-
526-This will match utilities like `tab-[1]` and `tab-[76]`.
527-
--
534-```css
535-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
536-@theme {
537-  --tab-size-github: 8;
538-}
539-
540:@utility tab-* {
541-  tab-size: --value([integer]);
542-  tab-size: --value(integer);
543-  /* prettier-ignore */
544-  tab-size: --value(--tab-size-*);
545-}
546-```
547-
548-This makes it possible to treat the value differently in each case if necessary, for example translating a bare integer to a percentage:
549-
550-```css
551-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
552:@utility opacity-* {
553-  opacity: --value([percentage]);
554-  /* [!code highlight:2] */
555-  opacity: calc(--value(integer) * 1%);
556-  /* prettier-ignore */
557-  opacity: --value(--opacity-*);
558-}
--
563-```css
564-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
565-@theme {
566-  --tab-size-github: 8;
567-}
568-
569:@utility tab-* {
570-  /* [!code highlight:2] */
571-  /* prettier-ignore */
572-  tab-size: --value(--tab-size-*, integer, [integer]);
573-}
574-
575:@utility opacity-* {
576-  opacity: calc(--value(integer) * 1%);
577-  /* [!code highlight:2] */
578-  /* prettier-ignore */
579-  opacity: --value(--opacity-*, [percentage]);
580-}
581-```
--
584-
585-Use `--default()` inside `--value()` to support a default value when the utility is used without an explicit value:
586-
587-```css
588-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
589-/* [!code word:--default(4)] */
590:@utility tab-* {
591-  tab-size: --value(integer, --default(4));
592-}
593-```
594-
595-This matches both `tab-2` and `tab-4`, as well as just `tab` which uses the default value of `4`:
596-
--
608-
609-The `--default()` function can also be used inside `--modifier()` to provide a default value when no modifier is present:
610-
611-```css
612-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
613-/* [!code word:--default(1)] */
614:@utility tab-* {
615-  tab-size: --value(integer);
616-  line-height: --modifier(integer, --default(1));
617-}
618-```
619-
620-This matches `tab-2/3` with a line-height of `3`, and `tab-2` with a line-height of `1`.
--
622-#### Negative values
623-
624-To support negative values, register separate positive and negative utilities into separate declarations:
625-
626-```css
627-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
628:@utility inset-* {
629-  inset: --spacing(--value(integer));
630-  inset: --value([percentage], [length]);
631-}
632-
633-/* [!code highlight:4] */
634:@utility -inset-* {
635-  inset: --spacing(--value(integer) * -1);
636-  inset: calc(--value([percentage], [length]) * -1);
637-}
638-```
639-
640-#### Modifiers
641-
642-Modifiers are handled using the `--modifier()` function which works exactly like the `--value()` function but operates on a modifier if present:
643-
644-```css
645-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
646:@utility text-* {
647-  /* prettier-ignore */
648-  font-size: --value(--text-*, [length]);
649-  /* [!code highlight:2] */
650-  /* prettier-ignore */
651-  line-height: --modifier(--leading-*, [length], [*]);
652-}
--
657-#### Fractions
658-
659-To handle fractions, we rely on the CSS `ratio` data type. If this is used with `--value()`, it's a signal to Tailwind to treat the value and modifier as a single value:
660-
661-```css
662-/* [!code filename:CSS] */
663:@utility aspect-* {
664-  /* [!code word:ratio, \[ratio\]] */
665-  /* prettier-ignore */
666-  aspect-ratio: --value(--aspect-ratio-*, ratio, [ratio]);
667-}
668-```
669-

